From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:20:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/19] fix(screenshot): wait for a frame's web fonts before
 capturing it

A frame render waited for `load` plus 120 ms. That does not cover web
fonts: a face served with font-display: swap (Google Fonts, which most
frames link) starts downloading at the first layout and its text is drawn
in the fallback face until it arrives. Each render runs in a fresh browser
context with an empty cache, so the download is real every time.

Measured on 24 frames of a real canvas that link Public Sans, loaded
through loadFramePage three at a time as thumbs.ts renders them, two
runs each (perf/fonts.mts): 13 and 5 of 24 were captured with a face
still loading. Their pictures show the fallback face, with different
glyph widths and line breaks from the same frame live in the editor, and
since a preview of the current version is cached as immutable the wrong
picture stays until the frame is next edited. With the wait: 0 of 24 in
both runs, and the load takes as long as before (median 586 and 544 ms
against 626 and 538 ms).

loadFramePage now waits for document.fonts.ready, bounded at 3 s as the
PDF export already does, so screenshots, previews and inspection all see
the frame's real typography.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 server/screenshot.ts | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/server/screenshot.ts b/server/screenshot.ts
index f7e5768..d3c3742 100644
--- a/server/screenshot.ts
+++ b/server/screenshot.ts
@@ -110,6 +110,25 @@ export async function openIsolatedPage(): Promise<IsolatedPage> {
   }
 }
 
+/** How long a render waits for the frame's web fonts before it goes ahead
+ *  with whatever has arrived (the PDF export's bound, pdfExport.ts). */
+const WEB_FONTS_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000
+
+/* `load` does not wait for web fonts. A face declared with font-display: swap
+   (what Google Fonts serves, and what most frames link) starts downloading at
+   the first layout, and its text is drawn in the fallback face until it
+   arrives. Every render runs in a fresh browser context with an empty cache,
+   so that download is real each time, and a screenshot taken 120 ms after
+   `load` often beat it: the picture then shows the fallback face, with other
+   widths and line breaks than the frame has in the editor. The PDF export
+   already waited (pdfExport.ts); screenshots, previews and inspection did
+   not. document.fonts.ready resolves once no face is loading. */
+async function webFontsSettled(page: Page): Promise<void> {
+  const timeout = new Promise<void>((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, WEB_FONTS_TIMEOUT_MS))
+  const ready = page.evaluate(() => document.fonts.ready.then(() => undefined)).catch(() => undefined)
+  await Promise.race([ready, timeout])
+}
+
 export async function loadFramePage(frame: Frame): Promise<IsolatedPage> {
   const loaded = await openIsolatedPage()
   const { page } = loaded
@@ -148,6 +167,7 @@ export async function loadFramePage(frame: Frame): Promise<IsolatedPage> {
       /* Slow external resources: inspect whatever has rendered. */
     }
     await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 120))
+    await webFontsSettled(page)
     return loaded
   } catch (error) {
     await loaded.close()
